+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running abagnale on Debian
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The port creates a user abagnale with home directory /var/lib/abagnale.
Maintenance should be done by switching to that user.

Preparing the database
======================

The database schema will transfer all ownership to the abagnale database role.
That role must be created before creating the database.

	# su - postgres
	$ echo "CREATE ROLE abagnale;" | psql

Create the database by loading the SQL dump:

	# su - postgres
	$ psql -f /usr/share/abagnale/database-postgresql.sql

The service then can be enabled and started by issueing:

	# systemctl enable abagnale
	# systemctl start abagnale

Maintenance
===========

The database should be vacuumed periodically by running abagnalectl vacuum.
The vacuum command of the abagnalectl maintenance application needs to be
performed by a role granted pg_write_server_files.

	# su - postgres
	$ echo "GRANT pg_write_server_files TO abagnale \
		WITH INHERIT TRUE, SET TRUE;" | psql

The package has setup an abagnalectl-vacuum service with corresponding timer.

	# systemctl status abagnalectl-vacuum.timer

By default the abagnalectl-vacuum.timer is setup to run daily.

The package also has setup an abagnalectl-dump service with corresponding
timer for creating database dumps.

	# systemctl status abagnalectl-dump.timer

By default the abagnalectl-dump.timer is setup to run daily.

Vacuum copies and database dumps will be written to the abagnale subdirectory
of the PostgreSQL state directory /var/lib/postgresql.


Publishing
==========

The abagnale.service by default runs with verbosity and plotting enabled using
the '-v' and '-p' command line options. Plots will be written to the services
state directory (/var/lib/abagnale).

The package has setup an abagnalectl-publish service with corresponding timer
for publishing plots.

	# systemctl status abagnalectl-publish.timer

Plots will be published to the services cache directory (/var/cache/abagnale).

Depending on the http software used the published plots can be served from
that directory. A configuration for the Apache 2 http server could contain an
alias like the following.

	Alias /abagnale /var/cache/abagnale/publish
	<Directory /var/cache/abagnale/publish>
		Options Indexes FollowSymLinks
		AllowOverride None
		Require all granted
	</Directory>

By default the abagnalectl-publish.timer is setup to run hourly.

Scaling
=======

The application can be distributed accross multiple machines in case a single
machine is too limited to handle a given amount of markets for given window
sizes. By starting the application with the command line option -I SAMPLES it
will not insert any tickers into the SAMPLES table. The -i command line option
needs to be set to a UUID used to separate state of different processes in the
database.

	# su -u abagnale
	$ echo "SELECT gen_random_uuid();" | psql -h database.fqdn ABAGNALE

Example
=======

Database Host: Does not perform any trading and is just used to populate the
SAMPLES table with tickers. It's configuration therefore only contains exchange
configurations with no trade statements. This host also runs the daily
maintenance. Without specifying an -i command line option the process uses a
hard coded default process UUID.

	$ cat /etc/abagnale/abagnale.conf
	dns-v4 udp://127.0.0.1:53
	dns-v6 udp://::1:53
	dns-timeout 3s
	exchange coinbase cdp-api-key /etc/abagnale/cdp_api_key.json
	exchange bitvavo api-key 9b1...
	exchange bitvavo api-secret 313...

Trading Host A: Initializes with tickers from the database. Reads tickers from
the Coinbase exchange. Does not write tickers to the database. Trades
everything Bitcoin at the Coinbase exchange.

	$ cat /etc/abagnale/abagnale.conf
	database target tcp:postgresql://database.fqdn/ABAGNALE user abagnale
	dns-v4 udp://127.0.0.1:53
	dns-v6 udp://::1:53
	dns-timeout 3s
	exchange coinbase cdp-api-key /etc/abagnale/cdp_api_key.json

	include /etc/abagnale/abagnale-coinbase-btc.conf

	$ cat /etc/abagnale/abagnale-coinbase-btc.conf
	trade at coinbase using trend return 0.0005 BTC window 36h\
		market match @BTC\
		demand-timeout-min 1h demand-timeout-max 9h\
		supply-timeout-min 1h supply-timeout-max 9h\
		stop-loss-delay 15m stop-loss-delays 2\
		take-profit-delay 3m take-profit-delays 1\
		take-loss-delay 18h take-loss-delays 1

	# systemctl edit abagnale.service
	ExecStart=
	ExecStart=/usr/bin/abagnale -v -p /var/lib/abagnale/plots \
		-I SAMPLES -i 98430c6b-4cf0-46c5-8d02-41dd7e93a481

	# systemctl restart abagnale

Trading Host B: Initializes with tickers from the database. Reads tickers from
the Bitvavo exchange. Does not write tickers to the database. Trades
everything Euro at the Bitvavo exchange.

	$ cat /etc/abagnale/abagnale.conf
	database target tcp:postgresql://database.fqdn/ABAGNALE user abagnale
	dns-v4 udp://127.0.0.1:53
	dns-v6 udp://::1:53
	dns-timeout 3s
	exchange bitvavo api-key 9b1... api-secret 313...

	include /etc/abagnale/abagnale-bitvavo-eur.conf

	$ cat /etc/abagnale/abagnale-bitvavo-eur.conf
	trade at bitvavo using trend return 0.25 EUR window 36h\
		market match @EUR\
		demand-timeout-min 1h demand-timeout-max 9h\
		supply-timeout-min 1h supply-timeout-max 9h\
		stop-loss-delay 15m stop-loss-delays 2\
		take-profit-delay 3m take-profit-delays 1\
		take-loss-delay 18h take-loss-delays 1

	# systemctl edit abagnale.service
	ExecStart=
	ExecStart=/usr/bin/abagnale -v -p /var/lib/abagnale/plots \
		-I SAMPLES -i de6924a0-1a23-4680-8780-175d0e345f75

	# systemctl restart abagnale

Performing database maintenance the abagnalectl maintenance application needs
to read a unified configuration containing all market setups.

	$ cat /etc/abagnale/abagnalectl.conf
	dns-v4 udp://127.0.0.1:53
	dns-v6 udp://::1:53
	dns-timeout 3s
	exchange coinbase cdp-api-key /etc/abagnale/cdp_api_key.json
	exchange bitvavo api-key 9b1...
	exchange bitvavo api-secret 313...

	include /etc/abagnale/abagnale-coinbase-btc.conf
	include /etc/abagnale/abagnale-bitvavo-eur.conf

Consider sharing the /etc/abagnale and /var/lib/abagnale directories using NFS.
